WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.080
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.319
I'm sorry if this video's a little bit disjointed,
the linings of my brain and spinal cord are

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:06.790
inflamed, which is pretty uncomfortable.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:10.250
Okay, so last week I sent out a message saying
that I was going to miss my Vlogbrothers video

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.320
due to hospitalization, and then I went back
to being in, like, the worst pain of my life.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.150
And then, while I was staring at the ceiling
begging for the pain to go away, the internet

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.840
diagnosed me with Ebola.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.800
Like, "John Green Ebola" was a trending search
on Twitter.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.240
People I knew in real life texted me and were
like, "Do they know what it is yet?

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.240
Is it confirmed?"

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.199
Then I was just like "Barf," and I don't mean
that figuratively.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:35.460
So ten days before getting what turned out
to be viral meningitis, I was in Ethiopia,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.960
which shares a continent with countries like
Guinea and Sierra Leone where there is currently

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:40.960
an Ebola outbreak.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.280
Now, never mind that Ethiopia is as far from
this outbreak as England is from Afghanistan.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:52.510
I had recently been on the same continent
as Ebola, and now I was in the hospital, ergo

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.510
Ebola.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:58.170
Quick side note, Hank, Ebola is a very serious
health crisis in West Africa, but more people

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:04.830
are going to die of malaria today than have
died from Ebola in the last ten years combined.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.320
So, anyway, Hank, when I went to the hospital
the doctors were very concerned, I mean, I

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.860
might have meningitis, or malaria, or typhoid,
or dengue fever, all of which can be fatal.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.960
But they weren't worried that I had Ebola.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.170
This is my point though, even if I had Ebola
it wouldn't have been a big deal!

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.840
I mean, I guess, it would have been a big
deal to me or my family.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.370
It wouldn't have been a big deal from a public
health perspective.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:31.140
Like, here's what the news would have said,
"An American has contracted Ebola.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.100
Shortly after returning from Africa, Ebola
Johnnie went to Orlando, Florida and signed

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.909
books for sixteen hundred people at a Harry
Potter convention, all of whom are now veritably

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:42.890
dripping with the disease."

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.130
But here's the thing, Hank, if I had somehow
magically gotten Ebola in Ethiopia, which,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:51.820
to reiterate, is impossible, I wouldn't have
given it to any of those sixteen hundred people

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.820
at LeakyCon.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.500
For one thing, you aren't contagious if you
aren't symptomatic, and also, I didn't share

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.930
bodily fluids with any of those sixteen hundred
people.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:03.030
Call me old fashioned, Hank, but I like to
keep bodily fluid sharing within my nuclear

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.030
family.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.120
Ebola is spreading because it's often being
treated in healthcare facilities with no running

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.980
water or latex gloves, which makes sterilization
functionally impossible.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.590
Just having water and bleach would prevent
a lot of these Ebola cases.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.290
This isn't some terrifying, uncontrollable
superbug.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.760
There's mounting evidence that there might
be treatments or vaccines or both within a

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.760
year.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.560
But the most important thing to remember,
Hank, is that this outbreak is happening because

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.060
of poor health infrastructure.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.050
Hank, I think we're obsessed with Ebola partly
because it's novel, partly because it's so

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:36.440
fatal, but partly because most diseases endemic
to the developing world seem Other.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.640
I mean, more than half a million people are
going to die of malaria this year, but unlike

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.670
Ebola, we don't think of malaria as a disease
that's "coming for us."

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.040
Whatever the hell "us" means.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.480
Now, of course, Ebola isn't coming for this
vague "us" either.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.980
It's not going to spread widely in the U.S.
or Europe.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.880
But, regardless, when Americans try to make
the Ebola outbreak about America, we do a

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.260
great disservice to ourselves AND to the communities
actually affected by this horrible disease.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:06.209
In Liberia and Guinea and Sierra Leone, health
workers have to choose between abandoning

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:09.730
their work and putting themselves and their
kids at risk for Ebola.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.530
And they have to make that choice because
of inadequate infrastructure and supplies.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:19.130
Addressing THAT problem instead of freaking
out about the disease coming to whoever "us"

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.590
is will actually reduce Ebola transmission.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:27.220
And, in the process, it will also save many,
many lives from malaria, and typhoid, and

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.379
also meningitis.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:31.000
Which brings me back to my infected brain
lining.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:37.010
Hank, it turns out that I almost certainly
got meningitis, not in Ethiopia, but in Florida.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.840
Oh, Orlando, city of my youth, city of my
inflamed meninges.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.560
Hank, it's going to take a little bit for
my meninges to completely de-itis.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.340
So, I apologize if I'm not around as much
in the coming weeks.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.690
But, nerdfighters, thank you so much for putting
stuff on your heads to cheer me up.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.370
It really did the trick when I desperately
needed it.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:56.590
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

